; THE REVERSAL NEED, RESTATED POLYMORPHICALLY — the only change from
; need-reverse-monomorphic.oath that matters.
;
; TWO laws, deliberately. Involution alone does not pin reversal: the identity
; function is an involution, and the corpus contains one that says so
; (bad-reverse). The second law — reversal antidistributes over append — is what
; separates them, but ONLY on the --implies surface:
;
;   --spec     does NOT separate them. bad-reverse STATES both laws, so the
;              content-hash surface returns it under BOTH, rendered "tested as",
;              with no guarantee column to rank it against reverse. This is
;              exactly finding #1 in the friction log: --spec cannot tell you
;              bad-reverse is FALSIFIED. (Its definition-level guarantee is
;              visible only via `oath ls` or the signature-probe fallback —
;              a different surface, not this one.)
;   --implies  IS what separates them: reverse is PROVEN on both laws;
;              bad-reverse is REFUTED on this one with a concrete countermodel.
;              Proof, not a label.
;
; The laws are stated at Int because that is the type this corpus's list laws
; are written at, and it makes the DISCOVERY cheap. What it does NOT do is prove
; reverse holds at Str: reverse is proven at List Int only (its own laws are
; Int-stated) and is not discharged parametrically, so an Int proof does not
; transfer to Str — see §11.2 of docs/experiments/issue-68.md, and finding #4 of
; the friction log. This query DISCOVERS the object (reverse is one hash whatever
; type you ask at); it does not license its use at Str. The consumer proves its
; OWN Str behavior separately, and only for the argument lengths it enumerates.
(defn wanted [a] [(xs (List a))] (List a)
  (prop involution [(xs (List Int))]
    (== (wanted [Int] (wanted [Int] xs)) xs))
  (prop antidistributes-over-append [(xs (List Int)) (ys (List Int))]
    (== (wanted [Int] (append [Int] xs ys))
        (append [Int] (wanted [Int] ys) (wanted [Int] xs)))))
